          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had reflux for years and now food sometimes sticks going down. How worrying is that, and what does guidance describe about when to investigate?",
          "answer": "Food sticking when you swallow is a new symptom, and it is worth raising with a clinician soon. Nothing in your description matched the emergency patterns I checked, but that check only covers a fixed list, so it is not a clearance. If swallowing becomes very hard, you cannot keep fluids down, or you have chest pain or breathing trouble, get urgent medical help now.\n\nThe guidance I could search says that difficulty swallowing (dysphagia) is not routinely part of uncomplicated reflux disease. Where it is present, it is recorded as an additional finding (K21 coding summary). Reflux guidance for adults with typical symptoms and no \"alarm features\" usually starts with an eight-week trial of acid-reducing treatment before any investigation (GERD initial management guideline). The guideline excerpt I retrieved is cut off before it lists those alarm features, so I cannot tell you which ones would change that approach. Ask your clinician whether your symptoms need investigation before a trial of treatment.\n\nThe retrieved material does not say how worrying your particular symptom is, or whether it points to a specific cause. Only an assessment by a clinician can answer that. Your clinician may want to know how long the sticking has been happening, whether it is getting worse, and whether you have weight loss or vomiting.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
